==================================================
EXECUTIVE ABSTRACT
==================================================

Recommended format:

PONS $101 conducted a registered token-launch experiment designed to observe public on-chain behavior following a human-authorized deployment.

The experiment recorded its hypothesis, configuration, announcement condition, network, protocol interface, unsigned payload, human approval, transaction result, public event data, early trading behavior, protocol-defined fees, and post-launch interpretation.

ROB — Research Observer for Bot-like Activity — reconstructed the launch and subsequent transactions using public blockchain evidence.

Behavioral classifications were intentionally separated from identity claims.

The experiment retained negative and inconclusive findings.

Any creator-fee entitlement and any actual treasury receipt were accounted for separately.

The complete package includes sufficient metadata, transaction references, schemas, code versions, hashes, and methodology for independent technical review.

==================================================
RESEARCH QUESTION
==================================================

PRIMARY QUESTION

Can a newly launched experimental token receive observable trading activity without advance public promotion?

SECONDARY QUESTION

When such activity occurs, can early launch behavior and protocol-defined fee mechanics be reconstructed accurately enough to support reproducible research?

PUBLIC-GOOD QUESTION

Can legitimate creator-fee receipts be transparently distinguished from estimated protocol activity and eventually routed through human-controlled governance toward documented public-good purposes?

